{"id":"connected-parties","title":"Organizations before these bodies with a link to the people voting","tier":"REDFLAG","question":"Which organizations and firms named as appellant, permit holder or applicant also gave to, lobbied, or are funded through the people who voted on their matter, and did they fare differently?","finding":"701 organizations and firms are named in these records; 95 match a name in a campaign, lobbying, City-payment or warning-sign file (a name match is not proof of identity), and 7 have a link to a member who voted on their matter in the two years before - 3 through donations to that supervisor’s committee, 5 as the client in a lobbyist’s reported contact. Counting each organization on each decided Board of Supervisors appeal, the linked ones were on the winning side in 4 of 7 and the rest in 20 of 102, too few to compare; recusals on record number 77 in commission minutes and 6 at the Board of Appeals, and supervisors were excused on 44 deciding appeal votes.","how":"Organizations are taken from Board of Supervisors appeal titles (the party after “on behalf of”), Board of Appeals minutes (appellant and permit holder with a company or association word in the name), Entertainment Commission minutes, legacy-business approvals and grant lists. Individuals, family trusts and individual artists are left out. Names are matched exactly after removing punctuation and company suffixes. Donations are contributions to a supervisor’s own candidate committee, by a donor who gave the organization as employer or by the organization itself, dated in the two years before that supervisor voted on the organization’s matter. Lobbying is a registered lobbyist’s reported contact with a member who voted, with the organization as client, on any subject, in the same two years. “City paid” is the total in this project’s profile of City-funded nonprofits (fiscal 2019 to 2026). The table lists every matched organization, those with a link to a voter first.","limits":" A donation by an employee is the employee’s own and need not reflect the employer. Exact-name matching misses organizations that act through a lawyer, an affiliate or a differently spelled name, and it can join two unrelated bodies that share a name. Commission minutes do not name applicants, and Board of Supervisors titles do not name project sponsors, so the project side is largely absent here; the next table tests it through addresses. Counts this small cannot show whether a link changed any outcome. A difference between areas, or between groups of cases, is not proof of bias. Areas differ in what is proposed, in zoning, lot sizes, building ages and land prices, and in who chooses to apply, object or appeal at all. ","columns":[{"k":"org","t":"Organization, as the record names it","type":"org"},{"k":"role","t":"Role in these records","type":"text"},{"k":"matters","t":"Matters","type":"int"},{"k":"result","t":"How they went for it","type":"text"},{"k":"gifts","t":"Donations to a voting supervisor, prior two years","type":"int"},{"k":"giftTotal","t":"Donations, dollars","type":"usd"},{"k":"giftTo","t":"Given to","type":"text"},{"k":"contacts","t":"Lobbyist contacts with a voting member, prior two years","type":"int"},{"k":"lobbied","t":"Members contacted","type":"text"},{"k":"contactsEver","t":"Lobbyist contacts with any official, any year","type":"int"},{"k":"cityPaid","t":"City paid this name, fiscal 2019 to 2026","type":"usd"},{"k":"flags","t":"Warning signs in this project","type":"int"}],"rows":[{"org":"Pacific Gas and Electric Company","role":"appellant to the Board of Supervisors","matters":1,"result":"lost 1","gifts":3,"giftTotal":600,"giftTo":"Alan Wong","contacts":71,"lobbied":"Alan Wong; Bilal Mahmood; Chyanne Chen; Connie Chan; Danny Sauter; Jackie Fielder; Matt Dorsey; Myrna Melgar; Rafael Mandelman; Shamann Walton; Stephen Sherrill","contactsEver":964,"cityPaid":null,"flags":12},{"org":"United Food & Commercial Workers Union Local 5","role":"appellan